WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Shortly after 6 pm on August the 4th, an explosion ripped through the port of Beirut.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.400
It killed more than 200 people, wounded over 6,500 and destroyed large parts of the city.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:22.900
Forensic Architecture was invited by Mada Masr to examine open-source information, including videos, photographs and documents,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.900
to provide a timeline and a precise 3D model to help investigate the events of August the 4th.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.900
The model is available via this link.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.400
The first photograph of the warehouse on fire was uploaded to Twitter at 5:54 pm.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:50.400
We carefully geolocated this photograph by identifying the key buildings, and calculated the camera's cone of vision.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:04.900
In this image we identified the location of the source of the smoke plume at the north-east corner of Warehouse 12.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.400
Smoke plumes are continuously transforming and have a unique shape at every moment.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:17.900
We modelled the plume at this crucial stage to help synchronise other videos without a timestamp.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:31.900
A video shot around the same time from one of the balconies of the residential tower building shows the same source of fire, clearly on the same side of the warehouse.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.900
The shape of the plume and the heat source suggest that the fire had evolved.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:51.400
Another video, starting at 5:58 pm from the nearby St George Hospital, provides ten minutes of uninterrupted footage of the warehouse.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.400
Within two minutes the smoke thickens and its colour changes to a darker shade.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:09.900
According to Gareth Collett, a UN explosives analyst we consulted, this suggests that the material burning inside the warehouse has changed.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:25.400
At 6:07 pm a new intense heat source appears on the other, north-western side of the warehouse, here, followed by a different, larger plume.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:32.900
The sparks that follow suggest the presence of small explosive charges such as fireworks.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.400
Thirty-five seconds later, at 6:08 pm, a large spherical plume appears above the centre of the warehouse.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:51.400
According to the explosives analyst, the symmetrical shape of the sphere suggests that it is a single-point explosion, originating in one particular place within the warehouse,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:59.900
and it is possible that as little as half of the 2,750 sacks of ammonium nitrate stored inside detonated.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.900
We use the shape of the two plumes from these explosions as metadata to synchronise the remaining footage.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.900
This video, taken from further back, provides an uninterrupted view of the events that followed.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.900
Within the span of nine seconds the spherical plume projected high into the atmosphere.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:43.900
Several tonnes of particulates thickened the air, and a red-coloured plume 755 metres high rose over the warehouse.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.400
We have thus identified four types of smoke plumes emanating from different parts of the warehouse within the space of these fourteen minutes.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.400
The first plume, at 5:54 pm, emanates from the north-east corner of the warehouse.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:06.400
The second plume, at 6 pm, is from the same source point but has a darker colour.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:13.400
The third plume appears on the north-west side of the warehouse at 6:07 pm.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:21.400
The final plume is developed from a spherical explosion located at the centre of the warehouse at 6:08 pm.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:32.900
Each of these smoke plumes, with their distinct shape and colour, provides indications as to the arrangement of goods in the warehouse, the way the fire developed and the layout of what was stored inside.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.900
A close-up examination helps in understanding the evolution of fire inside the warehouse.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:47.400
Early footage shows smoke leaking out of every opening, including the windows and the ceiling vents.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:56.900
From this point, at about 5:56 pm, the temperature inside the warehouse started rising rapidly.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.400
The smoke is visible as it changes colour to a darker shade in this footage from the east side.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.900
We stabilised the footage to reveal the full extent of the warehouse.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.400
The sound of fireworks starts being heard at approximately 5:59 pm.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.400
It shows that many windows and doors are shut.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:34.900
According to the expert, confinement creates hot spots, areas of high temperature in which ammonium nitrate can get close to its combustion point.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:47.900
As reported by media outlets, the fire brigade arrived approximately four minutes after an initial call was made to the station at 5:54 pm.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:56.900
In this footage, taken by the fire brigade on their arrival at the scene, the sound of fireworks continues to be heard.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:12.900
The 2,750 tonnes of ammonium nitrate were unloaded to this hangar in October 2014,

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:23.900
and as early as December of that year various port and customs officials warned of the dangers posed by its storage.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.400
Many subsequent warnings were issued.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:37.400
In February 2015, for example, a chemical forensics expert commissioned by the Lebanese courts to report on the state of the stored ammonium nitrate

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:46.400
described that 70 per cent of the sacks were torn open, their contents spilling out, and some of the crystals had darkened.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:53.400
Leaked images from February 2020 indicate that the storage conditions had not improved.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:59.900
The sacks were still torn open and their contents were still spilling out.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:07.900
The bay numbers visible in the ceiling allowed us to locate these bags in bays 9 and 10.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:15.900
The images show the presence of a container and a stack of wooden pallets.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:36.400
Another video, taken on December 18th and released by news outlet Al Jadeed, shows the state of ammonium nitrate bags stored at bay 6 and surrounding door 9.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:41.400
Stacked ammonium nitrate bags are blocking the entrance.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.400
Here we can see the numbers for bays 4 and 5.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:56.900
On the right of the videographer, between doors 9 and 10, a white-coloured wall is visible, suggesting the presence of a small service room.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:09.400
Together these videos and images allowed us to map a total of 243 bags of ammonium nitrate in the space.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:23.500
Given the location of the source of the spherical plume here, at bay 8, the remaining 2,507 bags of ammonium nitrate should have been stored here, occupying almost 2,000 square metres of space.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:33.400
But given the haphazard way the visible bags are stored, the space the entire stockpile occupied was likely larger.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:44.900
News reports suggest that in addition to the ammonium nitrate the warehouse also stored 23 tonnes of fireworks, 50 tonnes of ammonium phosphate,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:51.400
5 tonnes of tea and coffee, 5 rolls of slow-burning detonating cord and 1,000 car tyres.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.900
Each of these materials burns differently.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.900
The combustion of tyres, for example, produces a dark and thick plume.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:10.900
According to the explosives expert, it could correspond to the dark plume we had located on the north-east corner of the warehouse, so we placed the tyres roughly here.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:18.900
The expert also told us that the white plume that appeared on the north-west of the warehouse corresponds to the ignition of fireworks.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.900
We therefore placed the fireworks roughly in this corner.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:37.400
From an engineering perspective, this is the spatial layout of a makeshift bomb on the scale of a warehouse, awaiting detonation.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:43.400
According to Gareth Collett, ammonium nitrate is extremely difficult to detonate by fire alone.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:51.400
However, when confined and contaminated, this can lead to catastrophic detonation.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:58.400
It is sensitised by the presence of even the smallest quantity of additives and hence should be separated.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.900
We reviewed international standards for the storage of ammonium nitrate.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.400
Internationally accepted benchmarks include British regulations.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:14.900
According to British standards, stacks of ammonium nitrate must be limited to 300 tonnes,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.400
and each stack must be at least one metre away from walls and other stacks.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:26.400
Australian standards are more stringent.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:36.900
Bags must be arranged in 500-tonne stacks, but should be stored 890 metres away from the closest residential buildings.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:51.000
Using their equation to calculate safe distances, we can determine that a 2,750-tonne stack of ammonium nitrate should have been stored 1,570 metres away from the closest residential building.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.900
NASA's damage map illustrates the extent of the blast.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:14.900
All these regulations prohibit the storage of combustible or explosive materials, such as tyres or fireworks, in proximity to ammonium nitrate.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:23.400
This fact highlights the substantial and sustained state negligence which led to the formulation of a makeshift bomb.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:33.400
Around 1,000 of the survivors and victims' families have called for an independent investigation and public access to all relevant documents.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.900
As the search for political and economic accountability for the explosion of August the 4th continues,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:51.900
Forensic Architecture and Mada Masr are making the model, the geolocated videos and the source material used in the research publicly available via this link.
